:"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why does Priya say ''signs aren''t magic''?","options":["to argue that signs are too expensive for most city parks","to stress that information alone cannot replace listening to legitimate concerns","to suggest that visitors rarely read information in public spaces","to explain why ecological terminology should never appear on signs"],"answer":"to stress that information alone cannot replace listening to legitimate concerns","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-001-Q1","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"work","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-001","subskill":"nuanced_stance","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What is Professor Wu''s position on using metrics to assess collaboration?","options":["They are useful when combined with context and other evidence, but not as direct measures of collaboration.","They are more reliable than managerial judgement because they reduce subjective interpretation.","They are useful mainly for identifying employees who deliberately manipulate performance systems.","They should be abandoned because numerical measures inevitably distort how employees collaborate."],"answer":"They are useful when combined with context and other evidence, but not as direct measures of collaboration.","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-001-Q2","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"work","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-001","subskill":"implicit_meaning","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What does Wu mean when she says a measure can become ''a target''?","options":["People may alter their behaviour to improve the measured indicator rather than the underlying outcome.","Employees may refuse to collaborate if they know their activity is being recorded.","Managers may set an official minimum number of meetings for every employee.","A metric becomes useful only after the organisation publishes a numerical goal."],"answer":"People may alter their behaviour to improve the measured indicator rather than the underlying outcome.","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":2,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-001-Q3","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"work","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-001","subskill":"argument_structure","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why does Wu give the example of a team member brought in only at the end to fix problems?","options":["to demonstrate that late-stage problem solving is the most valuable type of team contribution","to prove that qualitative evidence is unnecessary when communication logs are available","to argue that employees should be included in every meeting from the beginning of a project","to show that activity data can sometimes reveal an important collaboration pattern when interpreted carefully"],"answer":"to show that activity data can sometimes reveal an important collaboration pattern when interpreted carefully","answerKey":"D","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-001-Q4","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"work","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-001","subskill":"speaker_attitude","difficulty":4,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"How does Wu characterize managerial judgement at the end?","options":["as useful only when a team has no measurable objectives","as unreliable because managers rarely agree with one another","as an outdated practice that should eventually be automated","as necessary but less comfortably measurable than a dashboard"],"answer":"as necessary but less comfortably measurable than a dashboard","answerKey":"D","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-002-Q1","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"general","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-002","subskill":"main_argument","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What is Osei''s main criticism of designing for an ''average user''?","options":["Designers often use the word ''average'' to avoid conducting more detailed research with users.","An average may describe a population without corresponding to any real person''s full set of characteristics.","Products designed from averages tend to cost more because they require unnecessary adjustments.","Average measurements are usually unreliable because designers collect data from samples that are too small."],"answer":"An average may describe a population without corresponding to any real person''s full set of characteristics.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-002-Q2","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"general","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-002","subskill":"inference","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What does Osei mean by saying good design often needs ''ranges''?","options":["The most successful products are those that can serve several unrelated purposes equally well.","Every product should be produced in enough versions to fit every potential user exactly.","Design should accommodate meaningful variation rather than optimise only for a central value.","Designers should replace numerical measurements with direct observation of individual users."],"answer":"Design should accommodate meaningful variation rather than optimise only for a central value.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":2,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-002-Q3","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"general","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-002","subskill":"nuanced_stance","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"How does Osei view the goal of inclusive design?","options":["It requires every product to work equally well for every conceivable user.","It is mainly a marketing strategy for offering several versions of one product.","It should identify and reduce unnecessary exclusion without pretending all constraints can disappear.","It should prioritise users at the statistical extremes over everyone else."],"answer":"It should identify and reduce unnecessary exclusion without pretending all constraints can disappear.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C1-002-Q4","level":"C1","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"technology","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"general","taskletExternalId":"L-C1-002","subskill":"implicit_meaning","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What changes when exclusion becomes ''a decision you can examine''?","options":["Users become legally responsible for choosing products that suit their needs.","Designers can justify, question or revise a limitation instead of treating it as an invisible default.","A product can be described as inclusive even when no changes are made.","Statistical averages become unnecessary because all design decisions are subjective."],"answer":"Designers can justify, question or revise a limitation instead of treating it as an invisible default.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-001-Q1","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-001","subskill":"nuanced_stance","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What distinction does Moretti draw between useful complexity and unhelpful complexity?","options":["Additional factors matter when they change the interpretation or weighting of causes, not simply because they make an account more detailed.","Any factor supported by evidence should be included regardless of its effect on the conclusion.","Complex explanations are useful only when they contain fewer than a certain number of causes.","A single cause is acceptable in public communication but never in historical scholarship."],"answer":"Additional factors matter when they change the interpretation or weighting of causes, not simply because they make an account more detailed.","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-001-Q2","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-001","subskill":"subtle_implication","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What does Moretti mean by saying clean explanations are ''portable''?","options":["They can be separated from moral judgement more easily than complex explanations can.","They are easy to repeat, remember and reuse across simplified forms of public communication.","They can be applied accurately to many different historical cases.","They remain valid when translated into several languages and cultural contexts."],"answer":"They are easy to repeat, remember and reuse across simplified forms of public communication.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":2,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-001-Q3","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-001","subskill":"rhetorical_function","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why does Moretti reject simply listing forty causes?","options":["Because long lists encourage people to search for heroes and villains.","Because explanation requires judging relative importance rather than transferring that judgement to the audience.","Because most events have only a small number of causes that can be empirically verified.","Because audiences are incapable of remembering more than a few historical facts."],"answer":"Because explanation requires judging relative importance rather than transferring that judgement to the audience.","answerKey":"B","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-001-Q4","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-001","subskill":"implicit_meaning","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why can contrary evidence feel like an attempt to ''weaken the lesson''?","options":["A moralised explanation can make nuance feel like a threat to the lesson people value.","Moral lessons become unreliable whenever an explanation involves more than one significant cause.","Scholars often introduce new evidence specifically to weaken the political force of public stories.","Historical evidence becomes less credible when it conflicts with a widely shared ethical judgement."],"answer":"A moralised explanation can make nuance feel like a threat to the lesson people value.","answerKey":"A","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-002-Q1","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-002","subskill":"conceptual_understanding","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What is Bell''s main point about silence?","options":["It is usually a sign that participants are afraid to disagree with authority.","It has become more difficult to interpret because most communication now occurs online.","Its meaning is interactional and context-dependent rather than merely the absence of words.","Its duration provides the most reliable evidence of what a speaker intends."],"answer":"Its meaning is interactional and context-dependent rather than merely the absence of words.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":1,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-002-Q2","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-002","subskill":"subtle_inference","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"Why does Bell compare a pause after an invitation with a pause after a factual question?","options":["to suggest that reluctance can only be expressed indirectly through silence","to argue that factual questions should be answered immediately in professional settings","to demonstrate that invitations produce longer pauses than questions in every language","to show that the same observable delay can carry different meanings depending on conversational expectations"],"answer":"to show that the same observable delay can carry different meanings depending on conversational expectations","answerKey":"D","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":2,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-002-Q3","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-002","subskill":"nuanced_stance","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What reservation does Bell express about telling leaders to ''give everyone space''?","options":["Anonymous tools should replace open discussion whenever people are likely to disagree.","Silence becomes useful only when all participants have approximately the same professional status.","Leaders often give junior employees too much unstructured discussion time in meetings.","The same ''space'' may feel less safe to enter for people with less power."],"answer":"The same ''space'' may feel less safe to enter for people with less power.","answerKey":"D","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":3,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"L-C2-002-Q4","level":"C2","skill":"listening","category":"Listening","topic":"society","genre":"expert_interview","audience":"adult","taskletExternalId":"L-C2-002","subskill":"implication","difficulty":5,"taskType":"multiple_choice","questionType":"listening","prompt":"What does Bell mean when she says technology ''rearranges'' the interpretive problem?","options":["Online communication makes silence less socially meaningful because participants are physically separated.","Digital tools can solve unequal participation even though they create new technical problems.","Different tools change the cues through which silence is interpreted without removing the ambiguity.","Technology turns ambiguous spoken silences into clearer written records that are easier to interpret."],"answer":"Different tools change the cues through which silence is interpreted without removing the ambiguity.","answerKey":"C","rubric":null,"sourceMaterial":null,"responseConstraints":null,"primaryEvidence":null,"taskletPosition":4,"bankVersion":"pilot-0.1","qualityStatus":"approved_for_pilot","psychometricStatus":"uncalibrated"},{"externalId":"LU-A1-001","level":"A1","skill":"language_use","category":"Language Use","topic":null,"genre":n